# Secret. Copy to ${SF_SECRETS_DIR}/database.env (outside the repository, 0600). Read by migrate, api, worker,
# backup, restore-check. Staging credentials only: never reuse production credentials (OPS-1).
# Default initial staging (self-hosted-db profile): host `postgres` on the private backend network, same
# credentials as postgres.env, e.g. postgres://USER:PASSWORD@postgres:5432/DB (no TLS inside the private network).
# Percent-encode reserved characters in USER and PASSWORD (e.g. "@" -> %40, "/" -> %2F, "?" -> %3F, "%" -> %25).
# Managed PostgreSQL >= 16 (target, V-04/V-15): private networking; TLS verified.
# Managed form:
DATABASE_URL=postgres://REPLACE_USER:REPLACE_PASSWORD@REPLACE_HOST:5432/REPLACE_DB?sslmode=verify-full
#
# sslmode=verify-full needs the provider's CA certificate. node-postgres and libpq do NOT use the system store
# automatically for a private provider CA: supply it as a file and name it in the URL, for example
#   ...?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/run/secrets/db-ca.pem
# (placeholder path: use your own). The file is not a secret but is installation-specific: keep it next to the
# env files under ${SF_SECRETS_DIR}, mount it READ-ONLY at that same path in EVERY container that reads
# database.env: migrate, api, worker, backup and restore-check (a compose override file with one `volumes:`
# entry per service). The same applies to RESTORE_TEST_DATABASE_URL in restore-test.env. How each provider
# publishes its CA is NEEDS VALIDATION at the managed-database rollout (V-04); never disable verification
# (sslmode=require without verification, or rejectUnauthorized=false) to get past a certificate error.
